1
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,930
This pencil tip is made of carbon.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:10,370
Cut it smaller and smaller,
down to single carbon atoms.

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:16,570
“Atom” comes from the Greek word
“atomos.” It means “can't be cut.”

4
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:22,480
About 2,400 years ago, Leucippus and
his student Democritus had an idea.

5
00:00:22,830 --> 00:00:26,140
Everything is made of
atoms and empty space.

6
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:28,360
Aristotle disagreed.

7
00:00:28,710 --> 00:00:33,030
He said everything is fire,
air, earth, and water.

8
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:37,190
Most people believed him
for almost 2,000 years.

9
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:41,430
How can we learn what's inside
something too small to see?

10
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,360
We do experiments: tests
that check an idea.

11
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:52,337
By 1800, chemists knew two laws:
rules nature always follows.

12
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:59,537
First, Antoine Lavoisier. When chemicals
react in a sealed jar, mass stays the same.

13
00:01:00,287 --> 00:01:02,417
Mass is how much stuff there is.

14
00:01:02,767 --> 00:01:08,877
Second, Joseph Proust. Water always has
8 grams of oxygen per gram of hydrogen.

15
00:01:09,227 --> 00:01:13,247
In 1803, John Dalton explained both laws.

16
00:01:13,597 --> 00:01:17,237
Atoms are tiny solid
balls that can't be split.

17
00:01:17,587 --> 00:01:21,817
All atoms of one element, or
pure substance, are alike.

18
00:01:22,167 --> 00:01:25,767
Atoms join in whole
numbers, never half an atom.

19
00:01:26,617 --> 00:01:31,297
This is the Solid Sphere model: a
picture that explains the clues.

20
00:01:31,647 --> 00:01:34,917
It predicts that there's
nothing inside an atom.

21
00:01:36,333 --> 00:01:40,493
In 1897, J. J. Thomson
tested a cathode ray tube.

22
00:01:40,843 --> 00:01:44,063
It's a glass tube with
most of the air pumped out.

23
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:48,743
A glowing beam shoots from the
negative end, called the cathode,

24
00:01:48,783 --> 00:01:51,423
to the positive end, called the anode.

25
00:01:51,773 --> 00:01:55,003
An object in the beam
casts a sharp shadow.

26
00:01:55,353 --> 00:01:57,853
So the beam travels in straight lines.

27
00:01:58,203 --> 00:02:00,773
The beam bends toward a positive plate.

28
00:02:01,623 --> 00:02:05,693
Charge comes in plus and
minus. Opposites pull together.

29
00:02:06,043 --> 00:02:09,933
So the beam is made of tiny
negative bits, called particles.

30
00:02:10,283 --> 00:02:13,043
A magnet bends the beam too.

31
00:02:13,393 --> 00:02:16,983
Thomson changed the metal,
and the beam stayed the same.

32
00:02:17,733 --> 00:02:20,583
So these particles are
in every kind of atom.

33
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:24,863
And they're over 1,000 times
lighter than a hydrogen atom.

34
00:02:25,213 --> 00:02:27,463
He had discovered the electron.

35
00:02:27,813 --> 00:02:33,233
Prediction: nothing inside.
Result: smaller parts inside!

36
00:02:33,983 --> 00:02:36,463
So the Solid Sphere model gets updated.

37
00:02:37,813 --> 00:02:40,513
Whole atoms have no overall charge.

38
00:02:40,863 --> 00:02:44,333
So something positive must
balance the electrons.

39
00:02:44,683 --> 00:02:48,283
In 1904, Thomson made
the Plum Pudding model.

40
00:02:48,633 --> 00:02:52,353
Electrons sit in a ball of
spread-out positive charge.

41
00:02:52,703 --> 00:02:54,823
Like blueberries in a muffin.

42
00:02:55,173 --> 00:02:57,443
Plum pudding is an old British dessert.

43
00:02:57,793 --> 00:03:02,583
Next time, a gold foil experiment
puts this model to the test.

